#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Make a place out of real seafloor.

Takes a bathymetric raster — NOAA's topobathy DEMs are what this was written
for — and produces the USD a dive is loaded from, plus a small record of what
the place is: where its datum is, how deep it goes, and where a dive should
begin in it.

The terrain is real. That matters more than it sounds: a seabed somebody
modelled by hand has the shape its author found pleasing, and every autonomy
tested against it learns that shape. A reef surveyed by a lidar has the shape it
has, including the parts that are inconvenient.

    tools/make-site kaneohe \\
        --raster ncei19_n21x50_w158x00_2021v1.tif \\
        --centre 21.4900 -157.8300 --across 1000

Units are metres throughout, and the output is Z-up with the origin at the
centre of the site and z=0 at the water surface. Depth is therefore negative z,
which is what the rest of this platform already assumes.
"""

